 1 big thing — Pope: Algorithms lack &quot;spark of humanity&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios Pope Leo XIV is calling for the Catholic Church to work with artists to protect human creativity amid a glut of AI-generated content, Axios' Herb Scribner reports. 

 🤳 Leo wrote on X : "In this era of artificial intelligence, it is becoming urgent to distinguish human art from what machines produce."

 "There is an ontological difference, even before an aesthetic one, between art and what a machine can generate through statistical calculation based on millions of images created by others." "Algorithms lack the spark of humanity." 🤖 Leo has spoken out about AI and its influence on humanity before.

 In the first encyclical of his papacy, he said there was a need to "disarm" the technology to prevent it "from dominating humanity." He approved the creation of an AI commission in May to coordinate the church's policies and work around the emerging technology. Recently, Leo warned of a "paradise of machines" invading and taking over our daily activities. He also said that warnings from AI researchers should be "taken seriously."

 He told reporters on Monday : "We have to keep working. We can't just sit back and pretend nothing is going to happen." Go deeper . 

 
 
